          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Originally, the
            %em Ginzburg–Landau model
            is a mesoscopic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} model
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconductivity"} superconductivity
          %p
            Roughly this type of model has then been used as models for 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %em Landau–Ginzburg model
            (LG-model) is a 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            characterized by the fact that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            contains a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/potential"} potential
            term: given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of the LG-model is schematically of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi LB
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi Σ
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi Φ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mi fermionic
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi terms
                    %mo )
                  %mi d
                  %mi μ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{LB} : (\phi : \Sigma \to X) \mapsto
                  \\int_\Sigma \left( \vert \nabla \Phi \vert^2 + \vert (\nabla W)(\phi) \vert^2
                  + fermionic\;terms \right) d \mu
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is the 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"}> dimension
            al
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W : X \to \mathbb{C}
            – called the model’s
            %em superpotential
            – is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+function"} holomorphic function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is actually taken to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            and all the nontrivial structure is in
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            Landau–Ginzburg models have gained importance as constituting one type of QFTs that are related under
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Fano variety
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Fano+variety"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            does not quite exist on it, since the corresponding supersymmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma+model"} sigma model
            is not conformally invariant as a quantum theory, and the axial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
            %span.newWikiWord
              R-current
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/R-current"} ?
            used to define the B-twist is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} anomalous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            of B-branes. A Landau–Ginzburg model is something that provides the dual A-branes to this under
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+mirror+symmetry"} homological mirror symmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            on a Fano variety. (…)
          %p
            A mathematical definition of the category of B-branes in the LG model is given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            (elaborated in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#KapustinLi"} Kapustin-Li, section 7
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaves"} coherent sheaves
            as in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-model"} B-model
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+complexes"} twisted complexes
            \: for these the square of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            is in general non-vanishing but rather a multiplication by
            %em superpotential
            of the LG-model, which therefore supplies a way to twist the (derived) category of coherent sheaves. The
            = succeed "-grading" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Z}
            = succeed "-grading." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Z
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Z}_2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+categories+of+singularities"} derived categories of singularities
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi λ
                  %msubsup
                    %mi D
                    %mi sg
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_\lambda D^b_{sg}(X_\lambda)
            (which is a finite product) where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_\lambda
            is a fiber over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
          %h2#properties Properties
